%% Two slabs, not one store. The mistake this diagram exists to prevent is the
%% assumption that "the contract's state" is a single dictionary. It is one
%% global slab owned by the application plus one local slab per opted-in
%% account -- different sizes, different lifetimes, different owners. The
%% asymmetry on the right is the point: the account controls its own slab and
%% can destroy it without the application's consent.
%% .
%% No subgraphs here on purpose. Mermaid ignores a subgraph's own `direction`
%% once the subgraph has edges crossing its boundary, which stacked the local
%% slabs into a 900x995 column on the first attempt.
flowchart TB
    G["The global slab<br/>one per application<br/>up to 64 key/value pairs<br/>size fixed at creation, forever<br/>outlives every member"]

    IN["OptIn<br/>creates that account's slab<br/>and raises that account's<br/>minimum balance"]

    L1["Alice's local slab<br/>up to 16 pairs"]
    L2["Bob's local slab<br/>up to 16 pairs"]
    L3["one more slab per<br/>opted-in account"]

    OUT["CloseOut runs your code.<br/>ClearState does not have to.<br/>Either way the slab is deleted<br/>and the app cannot refuse."]

    G --> IN
    IN --> L1
    IN --> L2
    IN --> L3
    L1 --> OUT
    L2 --> OUT
    L3 --> OUT

    classDef slab fill:#f2f2f2,stroke:#333333,color:#111111
    classDef edge fill:#ffffff,stroke:#333333,color:#111111
    classDef danger fill:#ffffff,stroke:#333333,color:#111111,stroke-dasharray: 4 3
    class G,L1,L2,L3 slab
    class IN edge
    class OUT danger
